          <span class="lr-cred"><a href="https://www.yelp.com/biz/steak-48-charlotte?adjust_creative=ncSIOsUpFaM6ZuPenKQYtw&#038;utm_campaign=yelp_api_v3&#038;utm_medium=api_v3_business_search&#038;utm_source=ncSIOsUpFaM6ZuPenKQYtw" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow" aria-label="Steak 48 photo on Yelp"><img src="https://topofcharlotte.com/wp-content/plugins/custom-business-directory/assets/images/yelp-logo-dark-bg.svg" alt="Yelp"></a></span>
                            </div>
        <div class="lr-main">
          <div class="lr-hd">
            <div class="lr-tt">
              <h3 class="lr-name">Steak 48</h3>
              <span class="lr-meta">Steakhouses &middot; Charlotte &middot; <span class="bb-pr">$$$$</span></span>
            </div>
            <span class="lr-score" aria-label="Insider Score 7.5 out of 10"><span>Insider Score</span><b>7.5</b></span>          </div>
          <p class="lr-hook">Charlotte&#039;s expense-account dining culture found its clearest expression in the banking boom, and Steak 48 is the room that fits that context most precisely.</p>          <span class="lr-order"><span class="u-lbl">What to order</span><span class="pl">Dry-Aged Ribeye</span><span class="pl">Lobster Tail</span><span class="pl">Truffle Mac and Cheese</span></span>          <span class="lr-cta">
            <a class="u-btn primary" href="https://topofcharlotte.com/go/book/steak-48/?z=board" rel="nofollow">Restaurant Reservations</a>            <a class="u-btn secondary" href="tel:9805804848">Call</a>            <a class="u-btn secondary" href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&#038;destination=35.1517066%2C-80.8270738" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">Directions</a>
          </span>
                  </div>
        </div>
                <details class="review-details" open>
          <summary aria-label="Review and visiting details for Steak 48"><span class="when-closed">Read the full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="when-open">Full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="detail-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
        <div class="lr-fold">
          <div class="lf-body">
            <h4 class="lf-hd2">Banking-money steakhouse that earns repeat visits</h4>                        <div class="lf-dishes">
              <div class="fd"><b>Dry-Aged Ribeye</b><p>The house centerpiece: dry-aged beef at its most straightforward, where the aging process does the seasoning work before the cut ever hits heat.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Lobster Tail</b><p>A steakhouse lobster tail signals the room&#039;s range beyond beef, drawing regulars who want surf alongside their prime cuts.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Truffle Mac and Cheese</b><p>Truffle mac anchors the steakhouse sides, a kitchen-tested comfort dish that earns its place on the table among the heavier protein orders.</p></div>            </div>
                        <div class="lf-prose"><p>Charlotte's expense-account dining culture found its clearest expression in the banking boom, and Steak 48 is the room that fits that context most precisely. The format is full-service, reservations-forward, built for the kind of night that requires a jacket and a reason to be there. Birthdays, client dinners, anniversaries: the operating character is special-occasion by design, not by accident.</p>
<p>The dry-aged ribeye is the anchor. Dry-aging concentrates flavor and tenderizes through controlled moisture loss, and the process requires time, space, and discipline from a kitchen running at volume. That the ribeye holds as the signature order speaks to consistent execution across a high-covers room. The lobster tail signals the steakhouse-seafood hybrid format common to the national upscale-steakhouse model, but it reads well in Charlotte, where the expense-account crowd expects surf alongside the turf. The truffle mac and cheese is the side that closes the deal for most tables: rich, indulgent, the kind of addition that turns a steakhouse dinner into something the table remembers.</p>
<p>The price is high-end and unapologetically so. Steak 48 is not the room for a casual weeknight. Charlotte's steakhouse lane has always run on banking money and corporate entertainment, and this room operates squarely in that tradition. The build-out skews modern rather than the dark-wood-and-leather register of older steakhouses, which tracks with the city's preference for new over nostalgic. The room draws regulars who return on milestone occasions, which is as clear a signal as any that the kitchen delivers consistently enough to merit the price on nights that matter. For a city that was still chain-dominated not long ago, a steakhouse that earns that kind of loyalty is worth noting.</p>
</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lf-rail">
                        <a class="lf-map" href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&#038;destination=35.1517066%2C-80.8270738" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener" aria-label="Directions to Steak 48"><img src="https://topofcharlotte.com/wp-content/uploads/rowmaps/436.webp" alt="Map showing Steak 48" loading="lazy" width="300" height="200"><span class="osm">&copy; OpenStreetMap</span></a>
