        </div>
                <details class="review-details" open>
          <summary aria-label="Review and visiting details for Midwood Smokehouse"><span class="when-closed">Read the full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="when-open">Full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="detail-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
        <div class="lr-fold">
          <div class="lf-body">
            <h4 class="lf-hd2">Frank Scibelli&#039;s BBQ anchor holds its ground</h4>                        <div class="lf-dishes">
              <div class="fd"><b>Smoked Beef Brisket</b><p>The anchor of the Texas-style program at this FS Food Group stalwart, brisket is the cut that earns Midwood Smokehouse its Charlotte regulars.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Baby Back Ribs</b><p>Baby back ribs land as the crowd-pleasing centerpiece of the four-item platters that tables tend to build their order around.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Pulled Pork Platter</b><p>Pulled pork is the Piedmont-leaning option here, worth pairing with a classic side like collard greens or mac and cheese.</p></div>            </div>
                        <div class="lf-prose"><p>Midwood Smokehouse sits at the top of Charlotte's barbecue conversation in a city that has been sorting out what its own BBQ identity means. It belongs to Frank Scibelli's FS Food Group, the same operation that opened Mama Ricotta's on Kings Drive back in 1992 and has since built one of the city's more durable restaurant groups. That context matters: this is a professionally run, multi-location concept, not a single-pit obsession, and the room delivers accordingly. Consistency is the house signature across the smoked beef brisket, baby back ribs, and pulled pork platter that anchor the menu. The brisket leans Texas-style, slow-smoked and sliced, which tracks with Charlotte's position as a barbecue melting pot.</p>
<p>The city sits in Piedmont country, where the tradition runs toward chopped pork shoulder, red dip, and red slaw, but brisket has become a legitimate part of the local conversation, and Midwood helped normalize that. The pulled pork platter keeps one foot in the regional tradition. The ribs split the difference, crowd-pleasing by design. None of it is a provocation; all of it works. Portions run large and the room operates at full-family volume, which explains the draw for group dinners.</p>
<p>The price point is moderate, which matters when the table has four people ordering meat by the pound. Charlotte's barbecue scene is still building its own narrative, and a bankable group concept like this one has played a real role in sustaining demand for smoked meat in the 704 through the transplant boom years, when plenty of newcomers arrived with no prior reference point for Piedmont style. The room is casual, loud in the good sense, and turns tables at a pace that suits the format.</p>
</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lf-rail">
                        <a class="lf-map" href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&#038;destination=35.221043811037575%2C-80.81486850357662" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener" aria-label="Directions to Midwood Smokehouse"><img src="https://topofcharlotte.com/wp-content/uploads/rowmaps/346.webp" alt="Map showing Midwood Smokehouse" loading="lazy" width="300" height="200"><span class="osm">&copy; OpenStreetMap</span></a>
                        <div class="lf-rows">
                            <div class="lf-row"><i>Hours</i><b>Mon - Thu: 11:00 AM - 9:00 PM</b><b>Fri - Sat: 11:00 AM - 10:00 PM</b><b>Sun: 11:00 AM - 9:00 PM</b></div>
                            <div class="lf-row"><i>Address</i><b>1401 Central Ave</b></div>              <div class="lf-row"><i>Phone</i><b><a href="tel:7042954227">(704) 295-4227</a></b></div>              <div class="lf-row"><i>Insider tip</i><p class="tiptext">The smoked beef brisket is the move here. Order it by the pound if the table is sharing, and ask for the red slaw to keep the Piedmont thread in the meal.</p></div>            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        </details>
              </article>
      <div class="featwrap" data-after="r10"><div class="promotion-block promotion-recommendation" data-recommendation="You may also like"><span class="promotion-label">You may also like</span><a class="v6-feat v6-feat--adx" href="https://topofcharlotte.com/event-tickets/"><span class="v6-feat__im" style="background-image:url(https://topofcharlotte.com/wp-content/uploads/feat/tickets-clt.webp);background-position:center"></span><span class="v6-adx__tx"><span class="v6-adx__k">Tickets</span><b class="v6-adx__h">Charlotte event tickets: every major venue, ranked.</b><i class="v6-adx__b">Arenas to theaters, ranked by what is actually on sale.</i><span class="v6-feat__btn" style="background:#c02a20;color:#ffffff">Find seats and dates</span></span></a></div><script>if(window.cbdRot)cbdRot(document.currentScript,"list-inline-10",[["advertise-trow","p:\/advertise","advertise",6],["tickets-city-trow","p:\/event-tickets","tickets",100],["u-lsn-trow","h:localsinglesnear.com","dating",100],["u-wi-trow","h:webigniter.com","marketing",100]])</script></div>      <article class="lrow" id="r11" data-rank="11" data-hood="Charlotte" data-price="$$$" data-tags="Upscale Dining|Romantic Spot|Local Favorite">
        <div class="restaurant-overview">
        <div class="lr-ph">
          <span class="lr-no">11</span>
                    <img src="https://media-cdn.tripadvisor.com/media/photo-o/18/ab/b9/d0/just-a-few-bottles-from.jpg" alt="The Crunkleton" loading="lazy" width="600" height="450" decoding="async">
          <span class="lr-cred"><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow" aria-label="The Crunkleton photo on Tripadvisor"><img src="https://topofcharlotte.com/wp-content/plugins/custom-business-directory/assets/images/tripadvisor-logo-dark-bg.svg" alt="Tripadvisor"></a></span>                  </div>
        <div class="lr-main">
          <div class="lr-hd">
            <div class="lr-tt">
              <h3 class="lr-name">The Crunkleton</h3>
              <span class="lr-meta">Cocktail Bars &middot; Charlotte &middot; <span class="bb-pr">$$$</span></span>
            </div>
            <span class="lr-score" aria-label="Insider Score 7.5 out of 10"><span>Insider Score</span><b>7.5</b></span>          </div>
          <p class="lr-hook">The Crunkleton operates in a register that Charlotte&#039;s banking-boom years made room for: upscale, unhurried, built around the bar as the main event rather than as an afterthought to the kitchen.</p>          <span class="lr-order"><span class="u-lbl">What to order</span><span class="pl">House-made Charcuterie Board</span><span class="pl">Pan-seared Duck Breast</span><span class="pl">Craft Old Fashioned</span></span>          <span class="lr-cta">
                        <a class="u-btn secondary" href="tel:7049190104">Call</a>            <a class="u-btn secondary" href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&#038;destination=35.2127277087423%2C-80.8182100000001" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">Directions</a>
          </span>
                  </div>
        </div>
                <details class="review-details">
          <summary aria-label="Review and visiting details for The Crunkleton"><span class="when-closed">Read the full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="when-open">Full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="detail-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
        <div class="lr-fold">
          <div class="lf-body">
            <h4 class="lf-hd2">Charlotte&#039;s Cocktail Bar With a Liquor Library</h4>                        <div class="lf-dishes">
              <div class="fd"><b>House-made Charcuterie Board</b><p>The anchor snack at a bar that takes its food program seriously, this board is the right call when the drink list demands something to work alongside it.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Pan-seared Duck Breast</b><p>A kitchen signal that The Crunkleton runs deeper than its cocktail reputation, the duck breast earns its place as the room&#039;s most substantive plate.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Craft Old Fashioned</b><p>The bar&#039;s calling card and the clearest way to take the measure of any serious cocktail program, ordered by regulars as the standard of comparison.</p></div>            </div>
                        <div class="lf-prose"><p>The Crunkleton operates in a register that Charlotte's banking-boom years made room for: upscale, unhurried, built around the bar as the main event rather than as an afterthought to the kitchen. The room's signature feature is a floor-to-ceiling liquor library with a rolling ladder, the kind of detail that signals the beverage program is taken seriously before a single glass arrives. The craft Old Fashioned is the standard-bearer here, and the execution holds up to the room's ambitions.</p>
<p>The kitchen runs a supporting role, but not a minor one. The house-made charcuterie board does what a well-considered bar program demands of its food: something to anchor the drinking without upstaging it. The pan-seared duck breast is the room's most substantial plate, the dish that earns the special-occasion designation and separates The Crunkleton from the stretch of cocktail bars that coast on atmosphere alone. Tomahawk Tuesday has built its own following, drawing a crowd specifically for that format on a night that might otherwise belong to weekday habit.</p>
<p>Charlotte's cocktail-bar category has grown alongside the banking-boom transplant wave; the 704 now has options that would not have existed here fifteen years ago. The Crunkleton sits at the upscale end of that spectrum, priced and positioned for date nights and occasions that call for a room that feels considered. The service reads as attentive rather than hovering, which is the right calibration for a room where the guest is meant to settle in. Walk-ins work, but on a weekend night the room fills and the pace reflects it. This is SouthPark-and-Uptown money spending on a bar that has done the work to deserve it.</p>
</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lf-rail">
                        <a class="lf-map" href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&#038;destination=35.2127277087423%2C-80.8182100000001" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener" aria-label="Directions to The Crunkleton"><img src="https://topofcharlotte.com/wp-content/uploads/rowmaps/464.webp" alt="Map showing The Crunkleton" loading="lazy" width="300" height="200"><span class="osm">&copy; OpenStreetMap</span></a>
                        <div class="lf-rows">
