          <span class="lr-cred"><a href="https://www.yelp.com/biz/brewers-at-4001-yancey-charlotte-8?adjust_creative=ncSIOsUpFaM6ZuPenKQYtw&#038;utm_campaign=yelp_api_v3&#038;utm_medium=api_v3_business_search&#038;utm_source=ncSIOsUpFaM6ZuPenKQYtw" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow" aria-label="Brewers At 4001 Yancey photo on Yelp"><img src="https://topofcharlotte.com/wp-content/plugins/custom-business-directory/assets/images/yelp-logo-dark-bg.svg" alt="Yelp"></a></span>
                            </div>
        <div class="lr-main">
          <div class="lr-hd">
            <div class="lr-tt">
              <h3 class="lr-name">Brewers At 4001 Yancey</h3>
              <span class="lr-meta">Brewpubs &middot; Charlotte &middot; <span class="bb-pr">$$</span></span>
            </div>
            <span class="lr-score" aria-label="Insider Score 7.2 out of 10"><span>Insider Score</span><b>7.2</b></span>          </div>
          <p class="lr-hook">Brewers at 4001 Yancey sits in the South End / LoSo corridor, the part of Charlotte that the LYNX line and a decade of redevelopment turned from light-industrial nothing into the city&#039;s most recognizable bar-and-brewery district.</p>          <span class="lr-order"><span class="u-lbl">What to order</span><span class="pl">Beer-Battered Fish and Chips</span><span class="pl">Wood-Fired Margherita Pizza</span><span class="pl">Braised Short Rib</span></span>          <span class="lr-cta">
                        <a class="u-btn secondary" href="tel:7044524001">Call</a>            <a class="u-btn secondary" href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&#038;destination=35.1865654%2C-80.8779978" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">Directions</a>
          </span>
                  </div>
        </div>
                <details class="review-details">
          <summary aria-label="Review and visiting details for Brewers At 4001 Yancey"><span class="when-closed">Read the full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="when-open">Full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="detail-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
        <div class="lr-fold">
          <div class="lf-body">
            <h4 class="lf-hd2">LoSo Brewpub That Built Its Own Gravity</h4>                        <div class="lf-dishes">
              <div class="fd"><b>Beer-Battered Fish and Chips</b><p>The beer-battered fish and chips put the brewpub side of the operation to work, the house beer pulling double duty in the batter.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Wood-Fired Margherita Pizza</b><p>The wood-fired margherita pizza is the anchor of a menu that leans on the in-house oven, straightforward in concept and built for a busy room.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Braised Short Rib</b><p>The braised short rib is the most deliberate plate on the menu, a low-and-slow format that stands apart from the casual brewery surroundings.</p></div>            </div>
                        <div class="lf-prose"><p>Brewers at 4001 Yancey sits in the South End / LoSo corridor, the part of Charlotte that the LYNX line and a decade of redevelopment turned from light-industrial nothing into the city's most recognizable bar-and-brewery district. The room has built a following that reflects exactly what that neighborhood asked for: a local brewery anchor with food serious enough to justify a full evening rather than a pit stop.</p>
<p>The kitchen leans on approachable execution with enough range to hold a table through multiple rounds. The beer-battered fish and chips is the kind of pub anchor that either earns its place or exposes a kitchen cutting corners; here it holds up. The braised short rib represents the upper register of what a brewpub menu can attempt, a slower-cooked, more deliberate dish that gives the room some credibility beyond snacks. The wood-fired Margherita pizza is the straightforward middle ground, though the crust runs thin and crackery, and portions of cheese have drawn notice as lighter than expected. The pizza program is functional rather than a destination order.</p>
<p>The room operates at volume. Tuesday trivia nights pack the floor to capacity, loud enough that conversation requires effort; the energy is genuine rather than manufactured, which is the better problem to have. The format runs in quick successive rounds, which suits a crowd cycling through pints more than one settling in for a long competitive night. Service holds across the room even when it fills.</p>
<p>At a moderate price point in a neighborhood thick with options, Brewers at 4001 Yancey earns its standing through consistency and room character rather than any single dish. Charlotte's brewery scene built itself one lease at a time after the Blue Line opened; this is one of the rooms that held.</p>
</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lf-rail">
                        <a class="lf-map" href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&#038;destination=35.1865654%2C-80.8779978" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener" aria-label="Directions to Brewers At 4001 Yancey"><img src="https://topofcharlotte.com/wp-content/uploads/rowmaps/403.webp" alt="Map showing Brewers At 4001 Yancey" loading="lazy" width="300" height="200"><span class="osm">&copy; OpenStreetMap</span></a>
                        <div class="lf-rows">
                            <div class="lf-row"><i>Hours</i><b>Mon - Sat: 11:00 AM - 10:00 PM</b><b>Sun: 11:00 AM - 8:00 PM</b></div>
                            <div class="lf-row"><i>Address</i><b>4001-A Yancey Rd</b></div>              <div class="lf-row"><i>Phone</i><b><a href="tel:7044524001">(704) 452-4001</a></b></div>              <div class="lf-row"><i>Insider tip</i><p class="tiptext">The braised short rib is the kitchen&#039;s most ambitious plate and the order that separates this from a standard taproom. Trivia nights run at capacity and loud, so arrive early or expect to work for a seat.</p></div>            </div>
          </div>
