          </span>
                  </div>
        </div>
                <details class="review-details">
          <summary aria-label="Review and visiting details for 44 Mills Kitchen + Tap"><span class="when-closed">Read the full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="when-open">Full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="detail-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
        <div class="lr-fold">
          <div class="lf-body">
            <h4 class="lf-hd2">Concord&#039;s New American Room That Overdelivers</h4>                        <div class="lf-dishes">
              <div class="fd"><b>Pan-Seared Salmon</b><p>The salmon anchors the lighter end of the menu, pan-seared to order in a room that runs toward heartier New American plates.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Braised Short Ribs</b><p>Braised short ribs represent the kitchen&#039;s more patient work, slow-cooked until the beef yields to the fork.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Shrimp and Grits</b><p>Shrimp and grits is the dish that earns its place on a New American menu in the Carolina Piedmont, the regional comfort standard by which a kitchen gets sized up.</p></div>            </div>
                        <div class="lf-prose"><p>Concord has spent the better part of two decades watching Charlotte build its restaurant scene from scratch, and rooms like 44 Mills Kitchen + Tap represent the suburban side of that same project: a moderately priced New American kitchen that punches above its zip code. The cooking here runs across the register that Charlotte's dining boom normalized, from polished seafood to low-and-slow braises, and the kitchen lands them with enough consistency to make this a genuine local anchor rather than a fill-in option.</p>
<p>The pan-seared salmon holds its own as a marker of execution: fish cookery is the first thing a kitchen gets wrong at volume, and this one keeps it in the win column. The braised short ribs read as the signature move, the kind of dish that shows whether a kitchen respects time, and the portions are substantial enough that the price-to-plate ratio tilts noticeably in the diner's favor. Shrimp and grits rounds out the program with the new-Southern language Charlotte's chef wave established, a reasonable touchstone for a Cabarrus County room drawing from the city's playbook.</p>
<p>The burger earns its own mention. In a category as contested as burgers across the broader metro, a consistent best-in-area claim from the local record is a specific distinction worth noting. The steak and eggs further signals that the kitchen works across dayparts and moods without losing focus.</p>
<p>The room runs busy but calibrates the noise floor well enough that a two-top conversation stays intact. That balance, a bustling house that doesn't become an endurance test, is harder to achieve than it looks and matters for the date-night and special-occasion crowd this kitchen is clearly after. For Concord, it is a room that would hold its own anywhere on the LYNX corridor.</p>
</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lf-rail">
                        <a class="lf-map" href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&#038;destination=35.41564%2C-80.6768" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener" aria-label="Directions to 44 Mills Kitchen + Tap"><img src="https://topofcharlotte.com/wp-content/uploads/rowmaps/420.webp" alt="Map showing 44 Mills Kitchen + Tap" loading="lazy" width="300" height="200"><span class="osm">&copy; OpenStreetMap</span></a>
