          <span class="lr-cred"><a href="https://www.yelp.com/biz/the-cowfish-sushi-burger-bar-charlotte?adjust_creative=ncSIOsUpFaM6ZuPenKQYtw&#038;utm_campaign=yelp_api_v3&#038;utm_medium=api_v3_business_search&#038;utm_source=ncSIOsUpFaM6ZuPenKQYtw" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow" aria-label="The Cowfish Sushi Burger Bar photo on Yelp"><img src="https://topofcharlotte.com/wp-content/plugins/custom-business-directory/assets/images/yelp-logo-dark-bg.svg" alt="Yelp"></a></span>
                            </div>
        <div class="lr-main">
          <div class="lr-hd">
            <div class="lr-tt">
              <h3 class="lr-name">The Cowfish Sushi Burger Bar</h3>
              <span class="lr-meta">Sushi &middot; Charlotte &middot; <span class="bb-pr">$$</span></span>
            </div>
            <span class="lr-score" aria-label="Insider Score 7.1 out of 10"><span>Insider Score</span><b>7.1</b></span>          </div>
          <p class="lr-hook">The Cowfish Sushi Burger Bar is a Charlotte original that turned a genuinely strange premise into a full-service room: burgers and sushi on the same menu, served together, without apology.</p>          <span class="lr-order"><span class="u-lbl">What to order</span><span class="pl">The Original Burgushi</span><span class="pl">Fire &amp; Ice Roll</span><span class="pl">BBQ Bacon Cheeseburger</span></span>          <span class="lr-cta">
                        <a class="u-btn secondary" href="tel:7043651922">Call</a>            <a class="u-btn secondary" href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&#038;destination=35.152541%2C-80.828092" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">Directions</a>
          </span>
                  </div>
        </div>
                <details class="review-details">
          <summary aria-label="Review and visiting details for The Cowfish Sushi Burger Bar"><span class="when-closed">Read the full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="when-open">Full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="detail-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
        <div class="lr-fold">
          <div class="lf-body">
            <h4 class="lf-hd2">Charlotte&#039;s Sushi-Burger Mashup, Built to Scale</h4>                        <div class="lf-dishes">
              <div class="fd"><b>The Original Burgushi</b><p>The Cowfish&#039;s signature creation: a burger patty rolled and sliced like maki, landing somewhere between a steakhouse and a sushi bar.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Fire &amp; Ice Roll</b><p>A roll built around heat and contrast, the kind of order that anchors the sushi side of a menu that refuses to pick a lane.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>BBQ Bacon Cheeseburger</b><p>The straightforward anchor for anyone who came for a burger, not the concept, and wants something familiar done without compromise.</p></div>            </div>
                        <div class="lf-prose"><p>The Cowfish Sushi Burger Bar is a Charlotte original that turned a genuinely strange premise into a full-service room: burgers and sushi on the same menu, served together, without apology. The concept launched here before expanding to other markets, which gives it a particular place in the city's dining timeline even as the room now reads more as a polished chain than a scrappy indie.</p>
<p>The signature move is the Burgushi, a construction that rolls burger ingredients into sushi form. It is exactly what it sounds like, and it works well enough that it functions as the room's calling card. The Fire and Ice Roll sits on the more conventional sushi side of the menu, representing a kitchen that handles both formats rather than sacrificing one for the other. On the burger side, the BBQ Bacon Cheeseburger is the straightforward anchor: a recognizable American format executed at a consistent level. The crab rangoon dip has developed its own following and operates as a starter that bridges both halves of the menu conceptually.</p>
<p>The kitchen runs at volume. The room is built for groups, families, and parties that cannot agree on a cuisine, and the size of the menu reflects that operating logic. Takeout execution has been uneven, particularly around temperature and portion consistency, which suggests the kitchen is calibrated for the dining room experience rather than to-go service.</p>
<p>Pricing runs higher than the casual setting might imply. The food quality generally supports the ticket on the sushi side; the burger side is less differentiated at that price point. For Charlotte, the room represents the banking-boom era of restaurant-as-entertainment concept: large, polished, crowd-tested, and built to handle a Saturday-night rush with no reservations drama.</p>
</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lf-rail">
