                <details class="review-details">
          <summary aria-label="Review and visiting details for New Zealand Cafe"><span class="when-closed">Read the full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="when-open">Full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="detail-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
        <div class="lr-fold">
          <div class="lf-body">
            <h4 class="lf-hd2">Sushi and Hibachi in a Casual Charlotte Room</h4>                        <div class="lf-dishes">
              <div class="fd"><b>Dragon Roll</b><p>The Dragon Roll is the crowd-facing centerpiece of the menu, a composed maki that draws regulars back through this Queen City sushi bar.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Hibachi Salmon</b><p>Hibachi Salmon bridges the kitchen&#039;s two registers, landing between the cooked hibachi preparations and the raw bar for diners who want something from the grill.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Spicy Tuna Crispy Rice</b><p>Spicy Tuna Crispy Rice plays the textural contrast angle, pairing the raw fish with a fried rice base that holds its crunch against the fish.</p></div>            </div>
                        <div class="lf-prose"><p>The name does nothing to telegraph what New Zealand Cafe actually is, which is a moderately priced Charlotte sushi and hibachi spot that has built a neighborhood following on rolls and grilled fish rather than any obvious regional identity. The Dragon Roll and Spicy Tuna Crispy Rice are the anchors of the menu and represent the room at its best: straightforward Japanese-American construction, executed well enough to draw regulars and road-trippers alike. The Hibachi Salmon rounds out the picture and gives the kitchen a second lane beyond raw and rolled, which matters for tables with mixed appetites.</p>
<p>The room operates casually, walks in as a local favorite, and positions itself for both date nights and family dinners without stretching too hard toward either. At moderate prices, that flexibility is the practical argument for the place. Charlotte's sushi category now runs to 34 listings in the database, so competition at this tier is real, and the question for any neighborhood sushi room is whether the execution clears a bar that keeps people returning.</p>
<p>The honest read on New Zealand Cafe is mixed. The signature rolls draw genuine enthusiasm, and for a room with no particular chef-driven provenance or Michelin consideration, that enthusiasm counts. But the rice quality and the handling of more demanding ingredients like eel have drawn pointed criticism, and those are not incidental complaints in a sushi context: rice is foundational, and overcooked eel signals a kitchen running on autopilot in spots. The Spicy Tuna Crispy Rice sidesteps that particular liability and remains the safest bet for a first visit.</p>
<p>The room fills a real slot in Charlotte's everyday dining map, the kind of neighborhood-level casual sushi that a transplant boomtown needs in volume. It is not a special-occasion destination, but at its price point and with its better dishes, it earns the traffic it gets.</p>
</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lf-rail">
