                <details class="review-details">
          <summary aria-label="Review and visiting details for 131 MAIN Restaurant"><span class="when-closed">Read the full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="when-open">Full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="detail-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
        <div class="lr-fold">
          <div class="lf-body">
            <h4 class="lf-hd2">Expense-Account Room With Kitchen Conviction</h4>                        <div class="lf-dishes">
              <div class="fd"><b>Pan-Seared Salmon with Seasonal Vegetables</b><p>The salmon anchors the lighter end of the menu, pan-seared and plated with whatever produce the season offers.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Dry-Aged Ribeye Steak</b><p>Dry-aged beef is the kitchen&#039;s most deliberate protein choice, and the ribeye is where that process shows most clearly.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Lobster Risotto</b><p>Lobster risotto sits at the top of the menu&#039;s register, the dish that signals how seriously 131 Main reads the New American room.</p></div>            </div>
                        <div class="lf-prose"><p>131 MAIN sits in the register Charlotte's banking-money crowd built the market for: upscale, polished, the kind of room that works for a closing dinner or a significant anniversary without requiring either party to explain why they're there. The city has always had demand for this tier; what separates the better rooms is whether the kitchen can back up the price point, and the evidence here is that it largely does.</p>
<p>The dry-aged ribeye is the anchor. Dry-aging is a commitment that shows up on the cost side of a kitchen's ledger before it shows up on the menu, and the cut reads as the thing the room is built around. Reviewers return to it specifically, which is the cleaner signal than general praise. The pan-seared salmon with seasonal vegetables occupies the other end of the protein ledger and holds up: the execution is consistent enough that it doesn't read as an afterthought for the table that doesn't want red meat. The lobster risotto fills the luxury-middle slot, the kind of dish that announces occasion-dining intent and, when it works, delivers the richness the price implies.</p>
<p>Charlotte's upscale tier has grown considerably since the LYNX boom and the banking-sector expansion filled SouthPark and Uptown with expense-account rooms. Some of those rooms lean on the occasion rather than the cooking. 131 MAIN reads as a room where the kitchen is doing real work: the protein-forward menu is focused rather than sprawling, and a focused menu is easier to execute consistently. The price is unambiguously upscale, so this is not an everyday room for most of the 704, but for the occasion it's designed for, the value proposition is clear. Service, per the record, matches the room's register.</p>
</div>
          </div>
