                <details class="review-details">
          <summary aria-label="Review and visiting details for Link &amp; Pin NorthCross"><span class="when-closed">Read the full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="when-open">Full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="detail-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
        <div class="lr-fold">
          <div class="lf-body">
            <h4 class="lf-hd2">Huntersville&#039;s Upscale Anchor With Serious Steakhouse Ambitions</h4>                        <div class="lf-dishes">
              <div class="fd"><b>Dry-Aged Ribeye</b><p>The dry-aged ribeye is the protein anchor here, drawing the kind of attention a steak earns when a cocktail bar takes its kitchen seriously.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Bourbon Glazed Salmon</b><p>Bourbon glaze on salmon bridges the bar program and the menu, a natural order in a room where the spirits list sets the tone.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Truffle Mac and Cheese</b><p>Truffle mac and cheese functions as the table&#039;s shared comfort dish, rich enough to hold its own against the main courses.</p></div>            </div>
                        <div class="lf-prose"><p>Link &amp; Pin NorthCross sits in Huntersville, north of the city proper on the Lake Norman side of the metro, positioned as the kind of room the suburbs have traditionally had to drive Uptown to find. The pitch is upscale occasion dining with a cocktail bar backbone: a modern build-out, expense-account pricing, and a menu that leads with a dry-aged ribeye as its clearest statement of intent. Dry-aging is a commitment, and the ribeye signals that the kitchen is reaching for something beyond neighborhood-casual territory. The bourbon glazed salmon fills the middle of the menu, a reliable pairing of sweetness and fat that suits the cocktail-forward program well.</p>
<p>Truffle mac and cheese rounds out the signature list as the kind of side that has become a shorthand for upscale American rooms across the 704 and beyond. The room operates at a register suited to anniversaries and business meals, the two occasions that keep a suburban room like this one financially viable. Service appears to be the consistent high point: the kind of attentive, occasion-aware front-of-house work that earns repeat visits from the SouthPark-and-north corridor. The cocktail bar framing is more than decorative; the beverage program is clearly central to the room's identity and not just a footnote to the food.</p>
<p>Charlotte's suburbs have grown sophisticated enough to support rooms with genuine steakhouse ambitions, and Link &amp; Pin is betting on that demographic. The tradeoff is the consistency question that follows any kitchen running at this price point: the ceiling is high when execution lands, but the gap when it doesn't is proportionally wide. The room is strongest when the kitchen delivers on its dry-aged promise.</p>
</div>
          </div>
