        </div>
                <details class="review-details">
          <summary aria-label="Review and visiting details for Pinky&#039;s Westside Grill"><span class="when-closed">Read the full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="when-open">Full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="detail-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
        <div class="lr-fold">
          <div class="lf-body">
            <h4 class="lf-hd2">Westside Burger Institution Since 2010</h4>                        <div class="lf-dishes">
              <div class="fd"><b>Pinky&#039;s Classic Burger</b><p>The house burger at this West Morehead spot, built in a former VW repair shop that opened in 2010, earns its place as the menu anchor.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Buffalo Wings</b><p>A starter that draws repeat visitors on return trips to Charlotte, grounding the bar-food side of the menu with straightforward heat.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Loaded Fries</b><p>The loaded fries here have become a reason locals and out-of-towners seek the place out specifically, stacked with toppings that make them a meal.</p></div>            </div>
                        <div class="lf-prose"><p>Pinky's Westside Grill opened in 2010 in a former Volkswagen repair shop on West Morehead, and the bones of that build-out have served it well: a neighborhood burger joint with enough character to pull out-of-towners back on return visits to the 704. The room runs loud and full, the kind of place where the energy announces itself before anyone sits down. Charlotte's burger scene is crowded with national concepts and fast-casual chains; Pinky's holds its ground as a genuine local room.</p>
<p>The Classic Burger is the anchor of the menu, straightforward in construction and consistent in execution. It is the kind of order that justifies the room's reputation as an everyday champ rather than a novelty stop. Buffalo wings are a reliable secondary draw, the sort of starter that sets the tone for a casual dinner built around sharing and staying a while. The loaded fries, which the kitchen runs in variations including a version with veggie chili, are the item that prompts people to plan around them on subsequent trips. That kind of dish specificity, where a side becomes a reason to return, is what separates a functioning neighborhood room from a forgettable one.</p>
<p>Service runs warm when the room is at its best, though volume puts pressure on the floor during busy stretches and drink refills can lag. Pricing is moderate throughout, which keeps the room genuinely cross-generational and family-friendly without tipping into the expense-account register that dominates Uptown and SouthPark. West Morehead is a different Charlotte than either of those corridors, and Pinky's fits the block honestly. For a city that was chain-dominant into the 1980s and has spent the decades since building an independent dining identity one lease at a time, a walk-in-friendly burger room with this kind of staying power is part of what that identity actually looks like.</p>
</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lf-rail">
                        <a class="lf-map" href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&#038;destination=35.2291284%2C-80.86747445979341" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener" aria-label="Directions to Pinky&#039;s Westside Grill"><img src="https://topofcharlotte.com/wp-content/uploads/rowmaps/347.webp" alt="Map showing Pinky&#039;s Westside Grill" loading="lazy" width="300" height="200"><span class="osm">&copy; OpenStreetMap</span></a>
