            <article class="lrow" id="r36" data-rank="36" data-hood="Charlotte" data-price="$$" data-tags="Local Favorite|Live Music|Cozy Atmosphere">
        <div class="restaurant-overview">
        <div class="lr-ph">
          <span class="lr-no">36</span>
                    <img src="https://media-cdn.tripadvisor.com/media/photo-o/12/f8/c6/e4/cajun-queen.jpg" alt="Cajun Queen" loading="lazy" width="600" height="450" decoding="async">
          <span class="lr-cred"><a href="https://www.tripadvisor.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow" aria-label="Cajun Queen photo on Tripadvisor"><img src="https://topofcharlotte.com/wp-content/plugins/custom-business-directory/assets/images/tripadvisor-logo-dark-bg.svg" alt="Tripadvisor"></a></span>                  </div>
        <div class="lr-main">
          <div class="lr-hd">
            <div class="lr-tt">
              <h3 class="lr-name">Cajun Queen</h3>
              <span class="lr-meta">Cajun/Creole &middot; Charlotte &middot; <span class="bb-pr">$$</span></span>
            </div>
            <span class="lr-score" aria-label="Insider Score 6.3 out of 10"><span>Insider Score</span><b>6.3</b></span>          </div>
          <p class="lr-hook">Cajun Queen occupies a Victorian house in Elizabeth, and that physical fact matters: Charlotte didn&#039;t have rooms like this when the city was still largely chain restaurants, and the building&#039;s age gives the place a weight most newer openings in the 704 haven&#039;t earned yet.</p>          <span class="lr-order"><span class="u-lbl">What to order</span><span class="pl">Crawfish Étouffee</span><span class="pl">Blackened Catfish</span><span class="pl">Shrimp and Grits</span></span>          <span class="lr-cta">
            <a class="u-btn primary" href="https://topofcharlotte.com/go/book/cajun-queen/?z=board" rel="nofollow">Restaurant Reservations</a>            <a class="u-btn secondary" href="tel:7043779017">Call</a>            <a class="u-btn secondary" href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&#038;destination=35.21455560141441%2C-80.82103323874894" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">Directions</a>
          </span>
                  </div>
        </div>
                <details class="review-details">
          <summary aria-label="Review and visiting details for Cajun Queen"><span class="when-closed">Read the full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="when-open">Full review &amp; visiting details</span><span class="detail-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary>
        <div class="lr-fold">
          <div class="lf-body">
            <h4 class="lf-hd2">Charlotte&#039;s Cajun Room, Live Music Included</h4>                        <div class="lf-dishes">
              <div class="fd"><b>Crawfish Étouffee</b><p>The crawfish étouffée is the room&#039;s benchmark dish, a Gulf-tradition stew that draws comparisons to New Orleans tables from regulars who know both.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Blackened Catfish</b><p>Blackened catfish is a Southern-river staple here, the preparation leaning on the Cajun spice work that defines the kitchen&#039;s identity.</p></div><div class="fd"><b>Shrimp and Grits</b><p>Shrimp and grits bridges Creole cooking with the Carolinas&#039; coastal-plain pantry, making it the natural crossover order in a Charlotte room built around Louisiana technique.</p></div>            </div>
                        <div class="lf-prose"><p>Cajun Queen occupies a Victorian house in Elizabeth, and that physical fact matters: Charlotte didn't have rooms like this when the city was still largely chain restaurants, and the building's age gives the place a weight most newer openings in the 704 haven't earned yet. The cuisine is Louisiana-rooted, and the kitchen runs the classics that define that tradition. The crawfish étouffée is the signature worth knowing about, a dish that holds up against the New Orleans original by most accounts from the room's regulars. The blackened catfish reads as consistent, prepared in the manner the technique demands rather than as a menu afterthought.</p>
<p>Shrimp and grits represents the Lowcountry adjacency that Cajun and Creole cooking shares with the Carolina coast, and the kitchen's version lands for the diners who order it. Live music is part of the operating format, not an occasional feature, which shapes the room's character on any given evening and sets Cajun Queen apart from the date-night options in Elizabeth and the broader midtown corridor. The price sits in the moderate range, which is appropriate for the neighborhood and the format. Not every visit produces the same result; a segment of the room's traffic finds the experience falls short of expectation, particularly on the execution side.</p>
<p>That is honest, and worth noting. The room draws people who have lived in Charlotte for years without visiting, then return. That pattern says something about how the room functions in the city's dining landscape: it holds a position no one else is particularly competing for, which is Cajun and Creole cooking with a genuine sense of place, in a building that predates the banking boom.</p>
</div>
          </div>
          <div class="lf-rail">
                        <a class="lf-map" href="https://www.google.com/maps/dir/?api=1&#038;destination=35.21455560141441%2C-80.82103323874894" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener" aria-label="Directions to Cajun Queen"><img src="https://topofcharlotte.com/wp-content/uploads/rowmaps/353.webp" alt="Map showing Cajun Queen" loading="lazy" width="300" height="200"><span class="osm">&copy; OpenStreetMap</span></a>
